    'The growth was real, and the context makes it modest. The same analyses placed water at just one to two percent of total climate technology investment. Set against a challenge that the UN describes as affecting billions of people and every one of its sustainable development goals, water remains structurally underfunded relative to energy, mobility and industrial decarbonisation.',
    'The reasons are not mysterious. Water is priced far below its replacement value in most markets, which suppresses the returns available to a technology that saves or treats it. Municipal buyers have long procurement cycles and low risk tolerance. Much of the demand sits with humanitarian agencies and governments in low-income countries — real need, difficult revenue. Venture capital has generally found cleaner stories elsewhere.',
    'The money that did move showed a pattern. Momentum continued into 2025, and dedicated water-focused funds emerged, including a $50 million second fund raised by Burnt Island Ventures for early-stage water technology. Capital increasingly favoured companies with technology already validated through pilot deployment, a route to recurring revenue via licensing or water-as-a-service, and the ability to scale across industrial, municipal or agricultural customers rather than a single segment.',
